    "https://jobs.oberlin.edu/postings/17710": {
      "title": "Jobs",
      "description": "Oberlin College & Conservatory Applicant Portal | Susan Duncan ’75 and Leo Fisher ’75 AMAM Curatorial Fellowship in Asian Art Skip to Main Content Jobs at Oberlin Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Contact Us Benefits Student Employment Susan Duncan ’75 and Leo Fisher ’75 AMAM Curatorial Fellowship in Asian Art Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening.  To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. All application materials must be submitted electronically through Oberlin College and Conservatory’s online application process at: https://jobs.oberlin.edu/ Applicant must be a current Oberlin College student who is not graduating during the spring 2027 semester. Please submit the following by 8/21/2026: 1) one-page cover letter explaining the applicant’s interest in the position 2) one-page resume 3) a 2-page (max) writing sample or excerpt from a longer piece of writing, preferably related to the visual arts 4) a list of your availability during museum business hours, Mon-Fri 9am-5pm (upload under \"additional document\") 5) unofficial college transcript 6) contact information for three professional references (at least two Oberlin faculty or staff) Review of applications will begin on 8/22/2026, followed by interviews for selected applicants, and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be addressed to: Dr. Kevin R. E. Greenwood, Joan L. Danforth Curator of Asian Art, kgreenwo@oberlin.edu Posting Details Announcement Information Job Summary This fall, the Allen Memorial Art Museum is launching three year-long curatorial fellowships, supported by the Susan Duncan ’75 and Leo Fisher ’75 AMAM Curatorial Fellowship Fund. One fellowship is available in each of the museum’s three curatorial collections (Asian art, European and American art, and Modern and Contemporary Art). The fellows will engage in curatorial research as well as provide administrative support for the curators. The fellowship is designed both to deepen research skills as well as to introduce students to critical aspects of the full range of curatorial work. In addition to working with the individual curators, the fellowship will also include career-centered conversations with other museum staff and other museum professionals as well as travel to northeast Ohio museums, conservation studios, and arts organizations. The fellowship extends over both semesters of the academic year at 12 hours per week as well as full-time employment in summer 2027, allowing time to create substantial, tangible outcomes to be part of a professional portfolio after leaving Oberlin. The aim of this program is to provide substantive support to the museum’s curatorial program and to provide depth of experience to set up students for greater success in the increasingly competitive market for curatorial positions. The fellows are expected to commit to the full arc of the fellowship, and students graduating in May 2027 are not eligible to apply. Responsibilities Responsibilities for the Duncan Curatorial Fellowship in Asian Art may include: ● General research on the Asian collection, including provenance research ● Updating records and creating lists on the internal collection database, The Museum System ( TMS ). ● Research and label writing for exhibitions ● Developing videos on the Asian collection ● Developing social media posts and public-facing materials ● Liaising with faculty and students who are coming to the AMAM to engage with the collection ● Art handling and pulling works from storage locations for study and exhibition preparation ● Other projects based on need or student interest Qualifications Applicant must be a current Oberlin College student who is not graduating during the spring 2027 semester. Applicants should have an interest in pursuing a career in museums or arts organizations. The successful completion of two courses related to art history or Asian studies, and an interest in the arts and cultures of Asia (West, Central, South, the Himalayas, Southeast or East Asia) are required. At least 100-level proficiency in an Asian language is preferred; however, applicants with native fluency in a major Asian language (such as Chinese, Japanese, Korean, Arabic, Persian, Turkish, Urdu or Hindi), but no art history or Asian studies background, will be considered. Applicants must have excellent research skills, strong writing skills in English, and be detail-oriented, responsible, and willing to take initiative. Applicants must have excellent organizational and communication skills, and the ability to work independently on both short and long-term tasks. Previous museum or gallery experience is a plus but is not required. Quick Link for Posting https://jobs.oberlin.edu/postings/17680 Compensation $16.00/hour based on actual hours worked Special Instructions to Applicants All application materials must be submitted electronically through Oberlin College and Conservatory’s online application process at: https://jobs.oberlin.edu/ Applicant must be a current Oberlin College student who is not graduating during the spring 2027 semester. Please submit the following by 8/21/2026: 1) one-page cover letter explaining the applicant’s interest in the position 2) one-page resume 3) a 2-page (max) writing sample or excerpt from a longer piece of writing, preferably related to the visual arts 4) a list of your availability during museum business hours, Mon-Fri 9am-5pm (upload under “additional document”) 5) unofficial college transcript 6) contact information for three professional references (at least two Oberlin faculty or staff) Review of applications will begin on 8/22/2026, followed by interviews for selected applicants, and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position may be addressed to: Dr. Kevin R. E. Greenwood, Joan L. Danforth Curator of Asian Art, kgreenwo@oberlin.edu Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Resume List of References Unofficial Transcripts Writing Sample Additional document Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Office of Human Resources Oberlin College 173 West Lorain Street Oberlin OH 44074 http://new.oberlin.edu/office/human-resources/ Phone: (440) 775-8430 Fax: (440) 775-8683 Hours: M-F 8:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. human.resources@oberlin.edu 2015 © Oberlin College | Privacy Policy To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.oberlin.edu/postings/17328": {
      "title": "CHEM 205 Teaching Assistant, Chemistry & Biochemistry",
      "description": "Oberlin College & Conservatory Applicant Portal | CHEM 205 Teaching Assistant, Chemistry &amp; Biochemistry Skip to Main Content Jobs at Oberlin Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Contact Us Benefits Student Employment CHEM 205 Teaching Assistant, Chemistry & Biochemistry Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening.  To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants must complete all of the “Availability” section of the application in order to be considered. Applications will be accepted until all positions are filled. Questions may be directed to Cristinel Mîinea, Laboratory Instructor and Manager (cmiinea@oberlin.edu) or Prof. Will Parsons, Associate Professor of Chemistry & Biochemistry (wparsons@oberlin.edu). Posting Details Announcement Information Job Summary The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry invites applications for Teaching Assistants (TAs) for the Fall 2026 semester. There are openings for assisting CHEM 205 lab sections. Applicants must be pursuing an interest in the sciences in order to be considered (pursuing a major in chemistry or biochemistry is preferred, but not required). Chemistry & Biochemistry TAs will get the opportunity to develop their understanding of the materials at hand, gain further knowledge of topics that they previously learned, develop a professional relationship with new students, and develop their skills as educators. Responsibilities 5-7 hour/week commitment. Responsibilities include: · Prior knowledge and reasonable mastery of CHEM 205 material; · Availability to attend TA meetings taking place once weekly; · Grading laboratory assignments in a timely manner; · Assisting students in lab with relevant techniques and processes. · With the agreement of TAs and instructors, additional paid hours may be available for tutoring sessions outside of lab periods, to assist students with preparation of lab reports, other lab assignments, and review of lab-related concepts. Qualifications You must be an enrolled student at Oberlin College & Conservatory to qualify. In order to be considered, applicants must have earned a passing grade in CHEM 205. Quick Link for Posting https://jobs.oberlin.edu/postings/17327 Compensation $13.00-$13.75 depending on prior TA work experience in the department. Special Instructions to Applicants Applicants must complete all of the “Availability” section of the application in order to be considered. Applications will be accepted until all positions are filled. Questions may be directed to Cristinel Mîinea, Laboratory Instructor and Manager (cmiinea@oberlin.edu) or Prof. Will Parsons, Associate Professor of Chemistry & Biochemistry (wparsons@oberlin.edu). Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Optional Documents Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Which afternoons are you available? (M-F 1:30-4:30 pm). (Open Ended Question) Office of Human Resources Oberlin College 173 West Lorain Street Oberlin OH 44074 http://new.oberlin.edu/office/human-resources/ Phone: (440) 775-8430 Fax: (440) 775-8683 Hours: M-F 8:00 a.m. - 5:00 p.m. human.resources@oberlin.edu 2015 © Oberlin College | Privacy Policy To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    }
  },
